          "section_id": "m01s01",
          "title": "The HSC course map and two-year bridge",
          "purpose": "Help students understand how the 120-hour Preliminary course prepares them for the 120-hour HSC course, while keeping the two stages distinct and recognising the three concurrent Year 12 strands of theory, Major Textiles Project/supporting documentation, and examination preparation.",
          "theory_html": "<h2 id='help-m01s01-course-map'>From Preliminary foundations to HSC study</h2><p>Textiles and Design is a two-stage Stage 6 course. The Preliminary course is 120 indicative hours and is the prerequisite for the 120-hour HSC course. This means Year 11 and Year 12 are connected, but they are not the same course repeated twice. Preliminary learning builds foundations that you carry forward, while HSC study expects you to use those foundations with greater independence and more developed reasoning.</p><p>In Preliminary study, you established knowledge in areas such as design, fibres, yarns, fabrics, textile testing, end-use requirements and the textiles industry. These ideas remain useful because they give you the language and concepts needed to interpret more complex textile situations. The important shift is that Year 12 is not about simply recalling what you learnt earlier. You increasingly need to select relevant knowledge, connect ideas and use evidence to support a decision or explanation.</p><p>A useful way to picture the two-year course is as a bridge. Preliminary learning provides the starting supports. HSC study asks you to move across the bridge by applying those supports to new decisions, explanations and judgements. Earlier knowledge remains valuable, but Year 12 should be understood through the expectations of the HSC course rather than treating Preliminary learning as if it were still the current course.</p><h2 id='help-m01s01-hsc-structure'>The HSC course map</h2><p>The HSC course is divided into four syllabus areas. Design accounts for 20% of the course, Properties and Performance of Textiles accounts for 20%, Australian Textiles, Clothing, Footwear and Allied Industries accounts for 10%, and the Major Textiles Project accounts for 50%. These proportions show how the course is structured, but they should not be imagined as completely separate boxes. Textile decisions often draw on ideas from more than one area.</p><p>Across Year 12, three learning strands operate at the same time. The first is <strong>theory</strong>, where you develop and connect knowledge across the syllabus areas. The second is the <strong>Major Textiles Project and supporting documentation</strong>, where design thinking and textile knowledge are applied through sustained project work. The third is <strong>examination preparation</strong>, where you practise interpreting questions, selecting relevant knowledge, organising ideas and explaining or justifying your reasoning.</p><p>These three strands support one another. Theory strengthens the knowledge you can draw on. Applied project thinking gives you experience in making textile decisions. Examination preparation develops your ability to communicate those decisions clearly in response to a question. Seeing the strands as concurrent helps you avoid the mistaken idea that one part of the course must be completed before another becomes important.</p><h2 id='help-m01s01-independent-decisions'>From knowing to deciding independently</h2><p>The main bridge from Preliminary to HSC learning is the move from knowing textile facts to using them to make supported decisions. In Preliminary study, for example, you may have learnt that fibre type, yarn structure and fabric construction influence textile properties and performance. In HSC study, you may need to use those relationships to judge whether a textile is suitable for a particular end use.</p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> imagine that a textile must perform well in an end use where durability and comfort are important. A basic response might simply name a fabric and state that it is strong. A more independent HSC decision begins by identifying the end-use requirements. You would then select relevant evidence about fibre, yarn or fabric characteristics, explain how those characteristics influence properties such as durability or comfort, and use that reasoning to justify whether the textile is suitable. The strength of the response comes from the connection between requirement, evidence, performance and judgement.</p><p>This same reasoning pattern can be used across the HSC course. In design, you can connect user needs to design decisions. In properties and performance, you can connect textile structure to end-use suitability. In industry study, you can connect evidence about the textiles sector to an explanation or judgement. The goal is not to remember every earlier note. It is to recognise which knowledge is relevant, use it accurately and explain why it matters.</p><p>A practical question to keep asking throughout Year 12 is: <strong>What does my existing knowledge allow me to explain, compare, justify or decide now?</strong> That question keeps the two-year course connected while still recognising that HSC study requires a more independent level of application.</p>",
