          "theory_html": "<h3 id='m10s03-end-use-profile'>Building an end-use profile</h3><p>Begin with Preliminary structure–property knowledge: fibre characteristics, yarn structure, fabric construction and finishes interact to influence observable properties and end-use performance. Selection therefore starts with the use context, not with a favourite fibre or fabric. An <strong>end-use profile</strong> describes what the textile must do, the conditions it may encounter and which properties matter most. Requirements can be organised as <strong>must</strong>, <strong>should</strong> and <strong>could</strong>. Must requirements are essential for the product to function. Should requirements improve suitability but allow some compromise. Could requirements are desirable when they do not undermine higher priorities.</p><p>Useful criteria should be observable or testable in principle, without assuming one prescribed test. Examples include whether a textile resists surface damage, bends without excessive stiffness, limits unwanted movement, manages moisture, retains shape or protects a contained object. Criteria should describe the required performance rather than naming a material in advance.</p><h3 id='m10s03-evidence-matrix'>Comparing evidence and trade-offs</h3><p>An <strong>evidence matrix</strong> places candidates against the same prioritised criteria so comparisons remain focused. Evidence might include direct observations, sample behaviour, known structural features or credible comparative information. A strong matrix separates evidence from assumptions. For example, a dense construction may suggest stability, but it does not prove abrasion resistance unless relevant evidence supports that claim.</p><p>Selection should consider the whole fibre–yarn–fabric–finish system. A strong fibre can still form a fabric that is too stiff for an intended use. A flexible knitted structure may improve conformity but provide less dimensional stability than another candidate. A finish may modify surface behaviour, yet it cannot erase every structural limitation. Trade-offs are therefore expected. A candidate may perform strongly on protection but less strongly on flexibility, or offer a comfortable surface while providing weaker shape retention. Weighting the must criteria most heavily prevents minor advantages from outweighing essential performance.</p><h3 id='m10s03-worked-justification'>Worked justification: protective storage sleeve</h3><p>Consider three generic candidates for a reusable protective storage sleeve. Candidate A is smooth, flexible and closely constructed. Candidate B is thicker and more structured but less flexible. Candidate C is soft and conformable with an open, more extensible construction. The end-use profile identifies three must requirements: protect the stored item from routine surface contact, remain sufficiently flexible for repeated handling, and hold the item securely. Easy cleaning is a should requirement, while decorative appearance is only a could requirement.</p><p>The evidence matrix shows that A appears strong in flexibility and surface smoothness, B appears strongest in shape retention and physical separation, and C conforms well but may allow excessive movement. No candidate dominates every criterion. A qualified selection could favour Candidate A because it currently offers the best balance across the three must requirements. That choice remains provisional. More evidence would be needed about durability during repeated use, how securely the sleeve retains its contents, and whether the surface continues protecting rather than marking the stored item. The justified claim is therefore relative: Candidate A is the strongest current choice for this end-use profile based on available evidence, not the universally best textile.</p><p>A defensible matrix also records unknown evidence rather than converting a gap into a guess. If a candidate meets two must requirements but has no evidence for the third, the gap should remain visible and influence confidence in the decision. Students can test the stability of their judgement by changing one priority or adding one credible observation, then checking whether the preferred candidate changes. This sensitivity check shows which evidence is decisive and helps distinguish a robust selection from one that depends on a weak assumption.</p>",
          "help_anchors": [
            "#m10s03-end-use-profile",
            "#m10s03-evidence-matrix",
            "#m10s03-worked-justification"
          ],
          "misconceptions": [
            "The textile with the greatest strength is automatically the best choice for every end use.",
            "A named fibre determines whole-product performance regardless of yarn, construction and finish.",
            "All selection criteria should be weighted equally even when some are essential.",
            "A justified selection proves that one textile is universally superior."
          ],
