          "section_id": "m13s01",
          "title": "Industry sectors and supply relationships",
          "purpose": "Map sectors and relationships without current-statistic invention.",
          "theory_html": "<h2 id='help-m13s01-industry-scope'>Australian Textiles, Clothing, Footwear and Allied Industries</h2><p>The Australian Textiles, Clothing, Footwear and Allied Industries can be understood as a connected system rather than a simple line of separate businesses. The system includes material supply, textile production, design and development, manufacture, distribution, wholesale, retail, services and end users. These sectors are interrelated because decisions made in one part of the system can affect requirements, timing, quality expectations and choices elsewhere. An organisation may operate in more than one sector, so a sector map should show functions and relationships rather than assume every organisation fits into only one box.</p><p>Relationships may occur locally, nationally or globally. A material supplier might provide inputs to a manufacturer in another region, while design information could be developed in Australia and communicated to production partners elsewhere. Distribution, wholesale and retail can also connect organisations across different locations. The important idea is not to memorise one fixed pathway, but to recognise how different functions connect for a particular textile product or service. Different products may therefore produce different maps while still using the same sector and flow concepts.</p><h2 id='help-m13s01-sector-relationships'>Sector relationships and flows</h2><p>A useful industry map shows more than the order of sectors. It should identify what moves between them. Material flows include fibres, fabrics, components, packaging or finished products. Information flows include design ideas, orders, forecasts, production updates, care information and customer feedback. Finance flows include payments between organisations and payments from customers. Specification flows communicate requirements such as dimensions, materials, performance expectations, quantities or presentation needs. Feedback flows return information about user experience, faults, preferences, demand or possible improvements.</p><p>These flows are often two-way. For example, a retailer may send sales observations or customer comments back to a distributor or product team, while the product team sends updated specifications or product information forward. A manufacturer may request clarification from a designer rather than simply receiving instructions. Showing two-way arrows can therefore make an industry map more accurate than a one-direction chain.</p><p>Consider a fictional Australian outdoor carry product. A supplier provides textile materials and components. A design team develops the product concept and specifications, then communicates requirements to a manufacturer. Finished products move through distribution to a retailer or service provider and then to end users. Money moves in the opposite direction through payments. Information also moves both ways: the manufacturer may report production constraints to the design team, the retailer may report customer questions, and users may provide feedback about function, appearance or care. That feedback can inform later design decisions, supplier choices or service information. The example is fictional, so it demonstrates relationships without making claims about any real organisation.</p><h2 id='help-m13s01-source-dated-evidence'>Using dated evidence for current claims</h2><p>Industry maps can explain relationships without using current statistics. However, a claim about present market share, employment, production volume, business numbers, industry status or another changing measure needs evidence from a dated primary source. The date matters because industry conditions can change. A current-sounding statement without a dated source should be treated as unsupported rather than assumed to be true.</p><p>When evaluating evidence, separate structural ideas from time-sensitive claims. The existence of sectors and flows can be mapped conceptually, while a numerical or status claim must be tied to evidence that identifies when the information applied. This distinction helps avoid presenting an old figure as if it describes the present.</p><p>A strong industry map therefore does three things: names the relevant sectors or functions, shows the material and non-material flows between them, and distinguishes stable relationship mapping from claims that require dated evidence. The goal is a clear network showing who or what connects, what moves between them, and where feedback can influence later decisions.</p>",
